WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 311: Henry VIII: Six Wives, the Break with Rome, and the Royal Navy

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:17.920
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the towering, tyrannical and mesmerizing monarch who changed the religious and political landscape of the English speaking world forever.

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.840
[Rebecca] King Henry VIII.

5
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:26.560
[Rebecca] Here is George with the grand, dramatic saga of Henry VIII.

6
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:35.920
[George] When 17-year-old Henry VIII ascended the throne in April 1509, Europe hailed him as the embodiment of the ideal Renaissance prince.

7
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.840
[George] Towering over six feet tall with auburn hair, broad shoulders, and an athletic physique.

8
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:57.520
[George] Young Henry was an accomplished archer who could draw a warbow at 240 yards, a fearless jouster, a gifted musician who composed hymns and ballads like pastime with good company, and a fluent Latin and French scholar who corresponded with Erasmus.

9
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:09.040
[George] Within weeks of his accession, Henry married his brother's widow, the pious and dignified Spanish princess Catherine of Aragon, obtaining a papal dispensation to legitimize the Union.

10
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:25.120
[George] To signal a new era of generosity, Henry executed his father's hated tax ministers, Empson and Dudley, and squandered the royal treasury on dazzling jousts, masquerades, and French campaigns, capturing Tournai and Therwon in 1513.

11
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:40.960
[George] In 1520, near Calais, Henry met King Francis I of France at the Field of the Cloth of Gold, an astounding 18 day tournament spectacle where fountains spouted red wine and temporary palaces of timber and canvas glinted in the summer sun.

12
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:49.120
[George] With Henry even challenging Francis to an impromptu royal wrestling match, Henry was the glittering darling of Western Christendom.

13
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:59.120
[George] Young Henry's court was the intellectual epicenter of Northern European humanism, attracting luminaries like Thomas More, John Collett, and William Grosson.

14
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:10.280
[George] Henry was a composer of genuine talent, writing three-part masses, motets, and lively consorts, playing the lute, organ, and virginals with virtuosic skill.

15
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.600
[George] His physical vigor was legendary.

16
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:29.000
[George] He would exhaust eight horses in a single day of hunting, dance through the night in velvet disguises, and win prizes in jousting lists wearing magnificent Greenwich forged steel armor etched with silver and gold, personifying the chivalric son king of a rising England.

17
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.600
[George] By 1527, the golden youth had soured into dark, brooding anxiety.

18
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:41.720
[George] Catherine had endured six pregnancies, but only one fragile child, Princess Mary, survived infancy.

19
00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:50.760
[George] Henry was convinced that God had cursed his marriage for violating the biblical prohibition in Leviticus against uncovering the nakedness of a brother's wife.

20
00:02:50.760 --> 00:03:04.360
[George] Concurrently, the king fell passionately in love with Anne Bolan, a witty, dark-eyed maid of honor educated at the French court, who refused to become his mistress, insisting she would only enter his bed as his lawful queen.

21
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:12.040
[George] Henry tasked his brilliant Lord Chancellor, Cardinal Thomas Walsey, with securing a papal annulment from Pope Clement V.

22
00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:12.600
[George] I.I.

23
00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.520
[George] But Walsey faced an insurmountable diplomatic obstacle.

24
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:28.360
[George] In May 1527, Imperial troops of Holy Roman Emperor Charles V, Catherine of Aragon's nephew, sacked Rome, holding the Pope virtual prisoner in the Castle Santangelo.

25
00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:35.480
[George] Charles V was not about to allow his aunt to be cast aside as an adulteress, and his cousin Mary declared a bastard.

26
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:46.240
[George] When Walsey's legatine court at Blackfriars in 1529 failed to deliver an annulment, Catherine fell to her knees before Henry in a heart-wrenching public plea for justice.

27
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:54.160
[George] Walsey was stripped of office and died awaiting trial for treason, leaving Henry in a furious, intractable impasse.

28
00:03:54.160 --> 00:04:01.520
[George] Catherine of Aragon's dignity at the Blackfriars Court in June 1529 was an indelible moment of royal drama.

29
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:07.680
[George] Walking across the hall, she knelt at Henry's feet, appealing to his conscience in accented English.

30
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.520
[George] Sir, I beseech you for all the loves that hath been between us.

31
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.160
[George] Take of me some pity and compassion.

32
00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.560
[George] She swore on her eternal soul that she had come to his bed an untouched virgin following Prince Arthur's death.

33
00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.200
[George] Henry remained silent on his throne.

34
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.720
[George] Walsey's failure to deliver the divorce sealed his ruin.

35
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:36.880
[George] Stripped of York Place and his cardinal's hat, he died on his way to the tower, lamenting that if he had served God as diligently as his king, he would not have abandoned him.

36
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:46.560
[George] Enter Thomas Cromwell, a self-made blacksmith's son from Putney, who possessed a legal mind of ruthless genius and radical evangelical sympathies.

37
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.240
[George] Cromwell presented Henry with a revolutionary solution.

38
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:57.920
[George] If the Pope refused to grant an annulment, the king should declare that the Bishop of Rome held no jurisdiction in England whatsoever.

39
00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:05.840
[George] Between 1532 and 1534, Cromwell guided a series of historic statutes through the Reformation Parliament.

40
00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:14.160
[George] The Act in Restraint of Appeals in 1533 famously declared that this realm of England is an empire governed by one supreme head.

41
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:27.280
[George] In January 1533, with Anne Bolan already secretly pregnant, Henry married her, and the new Archbishop of Canterbury, Thomas Cranmer, declared Henry's marriage to Catherine null and void.

42
00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:34.840
[George] In September 1533, Anne gave birth to a daughter, Princess Elizabeth, to Henry's bitter disappointment.

43
00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:43.640
[George] In 1534, the Act of Supremacy formally recognized King Henry VIII as the only supreme head in earth of the Church of England.

44
00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:56.280
[George] Those who refused to swear the oath of supremacy, including the saintly humanist Sir Thomas More and Bishop John Fisher, were publicly beheaded on Tower Hill, sending shockwaves across Catholic Europe.

45
00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:04.440
[George] Thomas Cromwell's administrative genius transformed the English monarchy from a personal feudal court into a modern department of state.

46
00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:12.440
[George] Through the Treasons Act of 1534, even speaking words against the king's marriage or supremacy was made punishable by death.

47
00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:19.480
[George] When Sir Thomas More stood trial in Westminster Hall, he was convicted on the perjured testimony of Richard Rich.

48
00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:33.080
[George] On Tower Hill, more famously declared on the scaffold that he died, the king's good servant, but God's first, leaving Henry with the blood of Europe's greatest intellectual on his hands and isolating England from the Catholic world.

49
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:36.360
[George] Anne Bolan's triumph was breathtakingly brief.

50
00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:44.360
[George] Sharp-tongued, intellectual and fiercely assertive, Anne clashed with Henry's courtiers and failed to produce the vital male heir.

51
00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:56.280
[George] In January 1536, Catherine of Aragon died, and weeks later, Anne miscarried a male fetus of three and a half months, an agonizing tragedy that sealed her doom.

52
00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:06.760
[George] Henry was already infatuated with Anne's meek, blonde maid of honor, Jane Seymour, and began convincing himself that his marriage to Anne had been cursed by witchcraft.

53
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:11.000
[George] Cromwell constructed a swift, devastating legal indictment.

54
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:23.600
[George] Anne was accused of adultery with five courtiers, including the court musician Mark Smeaton, Sir Henry Norris, and her own brother, George Bolan, Lord Rockford, along with conspiring the king's death.

55
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:30.400
[George] Though modern historians universally recognize the charges as fabricated, Anne and the five men were condemned.

56
00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:37.600
[George] On May 19, 1536, on a scaffold erected on Tower Green, Anne Bolan was executed.

57
00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:49.760
[George] In a final gesture of royal mercy, Henry spared her the agony of an English axe, hiring an expert swordsman from Calais who severed her neck with a single, lightning stroke of a two-handed blade.

58
00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.280
[George] Eleven days later, Henry married Jane Seymour.

59
00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.400
[George] Anne Boland's final days in the tower revealed an extraordinary dignity and poise.

60
00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:06.000
[George] She joked with the constable of the tower, Sir William Kingston, about her little neck and the skill of the French executioner.

61
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:18.960
[George] On the scaffold on Tower Green, clad in a robe of dark damask trimmed with ermine, she delivered a gracious speech praising Henry as a gentle and sovereign lord, asking the gathered crowd to pray for the king.

62
00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:26.800
[George] Her severed head and body were hastily placed inside an old elm chest that had held arrows and buried under the cobblestones of the chapel of St.

63
00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:31.840
[George] Peter ad Vincula, paving the way for Jane Seymour's immediate accession.

64
00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.680
[George] Jane Seymour finally gave Henry his long-sought miracle.

65
00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:43.600
[George] On October 12, 1537, at Hampton Court Palace, she gave birth to a healthy son, Prince Edward.

66
00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:50.080
[George] Tragically, Jane died of pure pearl fever twelve days later, leaving Henry genuinely heartbroken.

67
00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:53.040
[George] He would later request to be buried beside her.

68
00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Meanwhile, Cromwell unleashed the greatest land redistribution in English history: the dissolution of the monasteries.

69
00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:19.800
[George] Between 1536 and 1540, over eight hundred monasteries, abbeys and friaries were suppressed, their gold chalices and lead roofs stripped for the royal treasury, and their vast estates sold off to rising gentry and nobility, permanently tying the English aristocracy to the Protestant Reformation.

70
00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:35.000
[George] In Northern England, where traditional Catholic monastic charity was deeply cherished, forty thousand armed men rose in the pilgrimage of Grace in late 1536 under the lawyer Robert Ask, marching under banners showing the five wounds of Christ.

71
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:48.600
[George] Henry feigned conciliation until the rebels dispersed, then unleashed brutal martial law, executing Ask and two hundred northern rebels, hanging abbots from their own church steeples to demonstrate his undisputed supremacy.

72
00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.440
[George] The destruction of the monasteries was an cultural catastrophe.

73
00:09:52.440 --> 00:10:08.280
[George] Ancient monastic libraries containing centuries of Anglo-Saxon and medieval illuminated manuscripts were burned or used to wrap butter, stained glass was shattered, and architectural wonders like Glastonbury Abbey and Fountains Abbey were quarried for masonry.

74
00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:12.600
[George] The pilgrimage of Grace was the greatest domestic crisis of Henry's life.

75
00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:17.080
[George] Robert Ask's pilgrims took York and Hull without shedding a drop of blood.

76
00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:29.800
[George] Henry's ruthless deception, promising a northern parliament to hear their petitions before hanging Ask in iron chains from the battlements of Clifford's Tower in York, showed the terrifying ruthlessness of Tudor Majesty.

77
00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:41.000
[George] In 1540, Cromwell orchestrated a disastrous diplomatic marriage for Henry with the German Protestant Princess Anne of Cleves, relying on Hans Halbeen's flattering portrait.

78
00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:50.480
[George] When Henry met her in person at Rochester, he was repulsed, complaining bitterly that she looked like a Flanders mare and failing to consummate the union.

79
00:10:50.640 --> 00:11:01.840
[George] The marriage was swiftly annulled after six months, but the debacle allowed Cromwell's conservative Catholic enemies, led by the Duke of Norfolk, to engineer Cromwell's downfall.

80
00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:07.680
[George] In July 1540, the master architect of the Reformation was beheaded on Tower Hill.

81
00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:14.400
[George] That very day, 49-year-old Henry married 19-year-old Catherine Howard, Norfolk's niece.

82
00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:18.960
[George] Infatuated by her youth, Henry called her his Rose without a thorn.

83
00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:28.800
[George] But within fifteen months, evidence emerged that Catherine had engaged in premarital affairs and conducted a nocturnal liaison with royal gentleman Thomas Culpeper.

84
00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.280
[George] Catherine was beheaded on Tower Green in February 1542.

85
00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:50.400
[George] In July 1543, Henry married his sixth and final wife, Catherine Parr, a wealthy, twice-widowed intellectual who acted as a loving stepmother to Prince Edward and Princess Elizabeth, skillfully managing the king's foul tempers and nursing his ulcerated legs.

86
00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:57.920
[George] The tragedy of Catherine Howard was the pathetic consequence of court intrigue using a naive teenager as a factional pawn.

87
00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:11.520
[George] When Archbishop Cranmer slipped a paper detailing her past into Henry's hand during All Saints' Day Mass in 1541, the king broke down in uncontrollable sobs, refusing to believe his rose could be unchaste.

88
00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:18.640
[George] When the confessions of Culpeper and Dereham confirmed the scandal, Henry's sorrow curdled into homicidal rage.

89
00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:30.760
[George] Catherine Howard was beheaded on the very spot where Anne Bolan had died six years earlier, asking for the executioner's block to be brought to her cell the night before so she could practice how to lay her head upon it.

90
00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:34.360
[George] In his twilight years, Henry was a physical ruin.

91
00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:51.320
[George] Suffering from massive obesity, his waist measuring 54 inches, and tormented by agonizing chronic ulcers on his legs, likely resulting from a severe jousting accident in 1536, the king had to be moved through his palaces in a wheeled mechanical chair.

92
00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:54.200
[George] Yet his strategic vision remained potent.

93
00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:58.040
[George] Henry is rightfully remembered as the father of the Royal Navy.

94
00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:11.000
[George] He expanded England's fighting fleet from five ships to over fifty purpose-built warships, establishing royal dockyards at Dipped Ford and Woolwich, founding Trinity House, and casting hundreds of bronze cannon.

95
00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:25.400
[George] In July 1545, watching from Southsea Castle during a French invasion fleet's assault on Portsmouth, Henry wept as his flagship, the Merry Rose, suddenly heeled over and sank into the Solent with five hundred sailors.

96
00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:35.640
[George] On January 28, 1547, 55-year-old King Henry VIII died at Whitehall Palace, clutching the hand of Archbishop Cranmer.

97
00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:38.200
[George] Buried beside Jane Seymour in St.

98
00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:49.960
[George] George's chapel at Windsor, Henry left behind a radically transformed England, sovereign, naval-minded, religiously independent, and poised on the threshold of the modern world.

99
00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:54.280
[George] Henry's legacy was monumental, contradictory, and enduring.

100
00:13:54.280 --> 00:14:05.000
[George] He had transformed England from a second rate Catholic island into a proud, sovereign empire, forging the royal Navy that would defeat the Spanish Armada under his daughter Elizabeth.

101
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:07.560
[George] Yet the human cost was staggering.

102
00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:13.040
[George] Historians estimate that over 50,000 people were executed during his 38th year reign.

103
00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:32.080
[George] When his massive oak coffin was carried to Windsor Castle in February 1547, the lead lining reportedly burst overnight at Seion Abbey, where a stray dog was found licking the floor, a gruesome fulfillment of Friar Pito's prophecy that dogs would lick Henry's blood as they had Ahab's.

